The general rules of the doctrine of precedent in common law systems can be 
summarised as follows: 
• each court is bound by decisions of courts higher in its hierarchy; 
• a decision of a court in a diﬀerent hierarchy or lower in the same hierarchy may 
be persuasive but is not binding; 
• a court is not bound by its own past decisions but will depart from them with 
reluctance; 
• only the ratio decidendi of a case is binding; 
• obiter dicta (‘remarks in passing’, see/uni00A07.45ﬀ below) are not considered binding 
but may be persuasive; and
• precedents do not lose their force by lapse of time.

