Citing legal journals 
Journals, otherwise known as periodicals or law reviews, are a commonly used source of 
reference in legal writing.45 The conventional way of citing a journal article is as follows:
• the author’s initial(s) or given name(s) and surname;
• the title of the article in quotation marks;
• the year of publication (in round brackets);
• the volume number of the journal (and the particular issue within that volume);
• the title of the journal (not abbreviated) in italics; and
• the page at which the article begins (but not the last page).

